here we are in the Academy of Motion  Picture Arts and Sciences under the  Oscar celebrating kung-fu films for the  40th anniversary of end of the Dragon  hosted by the Academy  you must all be incredibly psyched to be  witnessing this I mean this is kind of a  remarkable thing I would say probably  over the age of five everyone in the  world today knows who Bruce Lee is  everybody knows what kung fu is I've  seen people in the most remote corners  of the earth pulling kung-fu moves and  making Bruce Lee sounds I've been to  places like Sadr City and Baghdad and  rural Transylvania where people have  never seen an Asian person before and  little kids will run to me and say Bruce  Lee Bruce Lee when you watch The Matrix  and they take the plug out of the back  of Neos head and he says I know kung fu  we all know what that means but 45 years  ago just before all these posters that  you've seen and before this movie was  made no one outside of Chinatown or  martial art circles knew what kung fu  was and bruce lee was just another  martial arts teacher and out-of-work TV  actor whose show was canceled struggling  to make a living so what happened within  five years kung fu had exploded onto our  cultural landscape there was a TV series  there were disco songs bubble gum cards  there was a hanna-barbera cartoon on  Saturday morning Elvis did martial arts  on stage as one of our panelists here  said suddenly there was a kung-fu studio  in every small town in America parents  took their kids to martial arts on  Saturday morning like they were taken in  a baseball practice this all seems  really normal to us all now but this was  completely new and strange in 1973  kung fu films didn't just change the way  Hollywood worked how we made action  films how we perceived a market of  foreign films they also changed the way  this country looked at Asians in all  minorities in the 1960s we were still  making yellow face movies in Hollywood  Mickey Rooney played a caricature  Japanese and breakfast at tiffany's with  big fake buck teeth and a crazy accent  and Bruce Lee played a butler he was a  kick-ass kung fu Butler but still he was  relegated to the role of a Chinese  Butler Bruce Lee didn't invent the kung  fu film Chinese filmmakers were making  kung fu films since the silent  Phil Mara you could say that martial  arts films are to China and to Hong Kong  what westerns are to America and  Hollywood it's the genre that is  uniquely Chinese it in particularly  embodies Chinese ideals and culture  Bruce Lee I'm sure you all know was  actually a pretty big child star in Hong  Kong and the Hong Kong film industry but  it was his hope and dream to become a  global star in Hollywood and for those  of us in this room in this business I  think what is particularly interesting  is that Hollywood couldn't figure out  what to do with Bruce Lee  when Bruce Lee said he wanted to be a  bigger star than his friends James  Coburn and Steve McQueen they laughed at  him not to his face but but they laughed  at him Bruce Lee knew all these people  he knew people the very center of our  business he knew the biggest movie stars  in the world he knew film producers here  the head of a major studio Warner  Brothers I don't know if you know this  but Roman Polanski hired Bruce Lee to be  his fitness trainer these people  respected his talents they recognized  his charisma but there was a belief and  it's amazing because it's actually  memorialized in internal memos that  there was simply no way a person of  color could ever be a movie star a major  box-office draw that's not the most  remarkable thing about the story I think  the most remarkable thing is that Bruce  Lee despite this persevered endured and  found a way to show his talent to the  world the huge success of the Bruce Lee  films in Asia and then around the world  caused Hollywood to take notice and to  Hollywood's credit they did what  Hollywood does best they instantly  change course 180 degrees showed Bruce  their love and got ahead of the wave and  overnight and this is amazing between  1972 and 1973 nearly 20% of the film's  release in American theaters were  kung-fu films you know if you look at  the picture of the Warner Brothers  marketing department in 1973 it looks  like the cast of the Lawrence Welk Show  they it was like Opie and Harry at  Warner Brothers and they were tasked  with releasing these new movies that  came from Hong Kong that were in Chinese  that had guys jumping around and  screaming and hacking each other they  had no idea what to do with it in the  file in the marketing file I found a  whipping from the New York Times about a  Warner Brothers screening in Harlem of  five fingers of death during the film  someone came into the theater with a 22  pistol and tried to rob the concession  stand and five members of the audience  incredibly amped up after seeing their  first kung fu film ran out beat the guy  up unarmed took his gun away from him  and held him until the police arrived  though the power of kung fu' the the  best thing the punch line for me is the  warners brothers marketing department  put a note in the clipping saying what  do we do with this should we were should  we release a press release this is the  press release should we should we  calling the press about this Bruce Lee  became an inspiration not just to me but  to generations of young minority actors  musicians filmmakers martial artists not  just because of his physical mastery but  because of his powerful defiance his  innate dignity his grace and his passion  Bruce Lee refused to be belittled he  refused to be marginalized and he  refused to be dismissed he commanded our  attention he commanded the frame he  demanded respect he had a power and  magnetism that few people have ever had  kung fu films changed America and they  changed the world so I want to say thank  you Bruce Lee thank you the producers of  Enter the Dragon thank you the Hong Kong  film industry and Warner Brothers all  the kung fu practitioners who toil and  obscurity and suffered making these  films and I want to thank the Academy  for preserving these posters from this  very special moment in our history thank  you thank you for coming today
 

